that batman producer william dozier had  also overseen the green hornet with  bruce lee being a die-hard kung fu movie  fan himself marco suggested to his  father that money could be made from the  old series larry used his connections  with fox to wrangle some 16 millimeter  prints of green hornet which he turned  over to marco marco then watched the  prince and assembled a rough cut that  combined footage of lee from the  episodes into a semblance of a new story  the completed film entitled the green  hornet opened in 1974 and grossed 45 000  in five days  enough footage from the green hornet was  left over for a sequel called fury of  the dragon which was released in 1976  and played in theaters for a decade  these films remain two of the most  lucrative and widely distributed  examples of what would be referred to  today as a  fan edit  there were a number of colorful film  industry figures distributing martial  arts films in the 1970s however  georgia-based michael thieves took the  colorful label to new heights or depths  as it were  in 1973 this scored a modest box office  hit after acquiring the rights to a 1971  taiwanese film starring wang yu called  the desperate chase which he retitled  blood of the dragon it was believed at  the time that this was an affluent music  producer from atlanta who made his foray  into distributing kung fu films  but after the fbi hit him with charges  of pornography and arson he gained a  reputation as a violent pornographer  using film distribution as a means of  laundering his money this was making  millions by overseeing his own peepshow  booth empire and wasn't above killing a  few people who stood in his way he even  went as far as placing a pipe bomb in  the car of a rival businessman thieves  went to prison in 1974 but used his  connections to georgia congressman  andrew young to secure transfer to a  minimum security prison in indiana from  which he escaped in 1978. after  murdering roger underhill a former  associate who had turned informant to  testify against him in 1978 thieves  earned a spot on the fbi's most wanted  list his time in the spotlight was brief  however the feds busted him for trying  to withdraw cash under a false identity  he even earned additional convictions  while serving a life sentence for  telling fellow inmates about the murders  he'd committed davis died in prison in  2013.  as with all film trends the first wave  of kung fu movies eventually wound down  in the 70s but not before the release of  one of the most outrageous asian action  films ever produced a 1974 article about  yakuza movies in film comment convinced  newline cinema president robert shea  that the next big martial arts picture  might come from japan instead of hong  kong a visit to tokyo's toei company did  yield a gangster movie called the  tattooed hitman but shea also spotted a  poster for an action thriller featuring  actor shinichi chiba shay screened the  film and was floored by chiba's near  maniacal anti-hero and astonishingly  violent set pieces especially one  particularly memorable scene featuring  the screen image turning x-ray to reveal  the full devastation of a fist crushing  a man's skull with ease shae bought the  rights to the film and gave it to  filmmaker jack shoulder for an english  language cut shoulder retitled it the  street fighter and dubbed its star sonny  chiba the mpaa slapped the street  fighter with an x rating for violence  which revolted most critics but  audiences flocked to the film and made  it a box office hit  new line released two sequels return of  the street fighter and the street  fighter's last revenge along with a  related film sister street fighter  featuring chiba and atsuko su-shihomi  other companies such as aquarius  releasing snatched up more cheaper  titles like the bodyguard to further  feed his fan base chiba who was not a  big fan of the street fighter himself  remained active in dozens of japanese  and us action films for decades chiba  tragically passed away from  complications of kovan 19 in 2021  there was a feeling that the martial  arts boom was beginning to tap out in  the mid-1970s as a result producers fell  back on another tried and true method of  bringing audiences back to theaters  folding kung fu into other genres  cross-pollination produced some  memorable experiments within the martial  arts genre jackie chan and samuel  samuhung scored big hits by folding  comedy into action but efforts to merge  kung fu with other genres weren't as  successful shaw brothers saw mixed  results by co-producing the stranger and  the gunfighter  the film was a martial arts spaghetti  western with lee van cleef and lolita  from five fingers of death shaw also  partnered with the uk's hammer studios  for the horror kung fu hybrid the legend  of the seven golden vampires from there  the mashups only got more dire shaw  teamed with warner brothers for the  blaxploitation action thriller cleopatra  jones and the casino of gold and enter  the dragon star jim kelly was stranded  in crummy martial arts sci-fi spy  oddities like death dimension efforts to  release kung fu in 3d such as the  taiwanese films revenge of the shogun  woman and dynasty only yielded minor  returns in the states chris pajali one  of the authors of the book these fists  break bricks sums up the genre hybrids  succinctly mixing martial arts with  comedy and horror seemed natural in hong  kong productions but attempts to combine  kung fu with other fading genres like  black's plantation and spaghetti  westerns even when the hybrids were  entertaining mostly came across like  acts of desperation  in the world that existed prior to the  internet it was much easier to pass  fiction off as the truth most people  simply had no viable way of verifying  claims and kung fu film distributors at  the time were all too willing to take  advantage of people's ignorance many  exploitation outfits weren't above  playing fast and loose with the facts  about titles the bruce ploitation  sub-genre is a perfect example as is the  practice of attributing fake awards to  their films and stars like the ebony  fist award won by playboy playmate jean  bell of tnt jackson the deadly knives  aka fists of vengeance never won karate  film of the year and china's grand  master of kung fu chiang ming li didn't  endorse iron fists because neither the  award nor the men are real sleazy film  distributors were always eager to  exploit new trends to promote their  movies this would often mean retitling  kung fu films to resemble contemporary  box office winners these included  chinese godfather kung fu exorcist and  kung fu halloween just to name a few  another entertaining trend was the  announcement of movie projects that  never existed the fact that no script  director or funding was in place didn't  stop boston-based producer distributor  seraphim keralexus from announcing enter  three dragons nor did it prevent ads  promoting the release of elsa meets  bruce lee in the devil's triangle that  picture would have brought together  diane thorne star of the infamous elsa  she-wolf on the ss and a bruce imitator  for some wacky hijinks unfortunately for  exploitation fans the ad is the only  element of the film that actually  exists  jackie chan's early career path was  pretty similar to bruce lee's both men  started out as child actors and chan  even worked as a stuntman and minor  player in lee's fist of fury and entered  the dragon after working his way up to  stunt choreographer jackie was tapped by  producer director lo wei to imitate  bruce in new fists of fury a phony  sequel to bruce's breakout feature  jackie later found his groove with  comedy action hybrids like 1978's  drunken master which led to his  ascension as a leading man after parting  ways with lil way jackie moved to golden  harvest where he created many of the  jaw-dropping action films that made him  a martial arts legend  just as bruce lee's success generated  the bruce platation scene jackie's  popularity spawned a similar explosion  of imposters doing their best to ape the  kung fu star's signature style of  comedic action chief among them were  indonesia's willie dozen also known as  billy chong and ulysses zen  also on the chan train korean actor  zhang jin wang who sported the  incredible pseudonym of elton chong in  one of the most perfectly reductive  mashups of all time the 1981 south  korean film jackie and bruce to the  rescue featured impersonators of both  bruce lee and jackie chan  theatrical screenings of martial arts  movies took a flurry of roundhouse kicks  in the early 1980s changing audience  tastes headlines linking kung fu to real  life violence and the collapse of the  grindhouse theater circuits eliminated  many of the venues that played asian  action films mel marin of cinema shares  and world northal kept many older titles  in circulation through black belt  theater packages for television but if  you wanted to see the latest martial  arts movies in the 80s your choices were  limited your only options were going to  chinese language theaters in big cities  to see the new jackie chan titles  ponying up exorbitant fees for video  cassettes or settling for the action  stylings of chuck norris  norris operated on the fringes of the  film industry throughout the early 70s  including a face-off against his friend  bruce lee in way of the dragon however  he vaulted to leading man status with a  string of low budget action films  beginning in 1977 with breaker breaker  from there he enjoyed box office success  that extended into the early 1980s  attention from major studios soon  followed as did a second wave of stardom  for norris as a mainstream star in films  like missing in action of course once  norse's character started focusing more  on guns than kicks someone had to take  his place many of these would-be heroes  were cut from the same cloth as norris  young white men with martial arts  backgrounds like kickboxing champion joe  lewis lauren avadon and kurt mckenny  each got a shot at kung fu stardom in  films like mckinney in no retreat no  surrender and avadon in king of the  kickboxers but they were quickly  supplanted by a new crop of stars many  of whom shared the screen with them in  supporting roles  this era produced a few more  recognizable names pojolli wrote in  these fists break bricks  it wasn't until the close of the 80s  when solid box office straws like  jean-claude van damme and steven seagal  filled the void left by norris while  cynthia rothrock billy blanks and don  the dragon wilson became direct to video  action attractions  martial arts found a comfortable second  home on vhs in the 1980s older or  obscure titles gained new audiences  through vhs releases and the demand for  new material gave acting and directing  hopefuls a shot at making and releasing  their dream projects it seemed like  anything that was feature-length and  somewhat confidently produced could make  money in the home video market that's  that's great mister really it's that's  just what i needed  many of these home-grown efforts came  from places outside of hollywood  missouri-based detective and martial  arts instructor ron white threw down a  little over twelve thousand dollars to  make 1985's justice ninja style in which  he starred as a small town ninja jun  chung who starred in bruce lee fights  back from the grave made several ultra  low budget ninja picks including la  street fighters which featured the 41  year old chong as a high school gang  leader chuck mcneil directed a slew of  home movie style action films like  dragon spade and dragon from the east  though the actual existence of several  of his films is unclear despite  challenges and critics these regional  filmmakers remain undaunted  wrote in these fist break bricks they  delivered low budget cult classics like  the chicken obsessed furious and grand  master yk kim's ninja rific the miami  connection and oddities like wichita's  king kung fu about an escaped kung fu  fighting gorilla who abducts a pizza hut  waitress and skills the tallest holiday  inn in town  the ninja craze in the united states was  at full spin in 1985. the teenage mutant  ninja turtles had just burst out of the  sewers and onto the scene toy stores  were packed to the brim with remco's  ninja strike force action figures and  ninjas were the cutting edge of fashion  for halloween that year ninjas had been  on the sideline of movies and television  for decades like in the 1967 bond  feature you only lived twice it was the  release of eric van lusbader's 1980  novel the ninja that sparked mainstream  interest in all things stealthy and  japanese  this newly stoked interest in the  clandestine warriors brought about canon  films enter the ninja in 1980 which  featured franco nero the original django  of spaghetti western fame playing a  ninja doing battle against an evil  businessman few found nero believable in  the role but the top ras at canon films  found a new star in show kosuji who  played an evil ninja a martial arts  teacher and minor actor in bruce  ploytation films kosiji briefly became  the go-to on-screen ninja in the 1980s  still his early showcases for canon were  underwhelming or in the case of ninja 3  the domination downright bizarre he  parted ways with the company in the  mid-80s to oversee his own films for  trans world entertainment including the  box office hit pray for death  canon films rebounded with american  ninja a vehicle intended for chuck  norris which instead became a  star-making project for formal model  michael darkoff  dukkhaff stars is a rather conspicuous  white guy who just so happens to find  out he is descended from a long line of  ninja masters this ludicrous effort was  followed by four more american ninja  titles regardless of how off the rails  some ninja films were getting ninjas  retained their hold on martial arts fans  and pop culture through toys costumes  and video games through the early 1990s  in a sub-genre where plot cohesion often  took a backseat to smoke bombs and  color-coordinated costumes the ninja  films of director godfrey ho stand out  as some of the most confusing  haphazardly made titles in the martial  arts genre  [Music]  hoe cut his filmmaking teeth at shaw  brothers before teaming with producers  joseph lai and thomas tang of  intercontinental film distributors or  ifd together they went on to crank out  dozens of low-budget action films in  international markets that were largely  ignored by other hong kong filmmakers  the success of enter the ninja spurred  ifd to produce their own ninja films  many starred american actor richard  harrison who had been a major player in  italian westerns and spy films in the  1960s it didn't matter that harrison had  no apparent martial arts abilities the  full-body ninja costumes allowed for  extensive use of body doubles during  action scenes even though harrison  technically appeared in four ifd ninja  films including 1984's scorpion  thunderbolt he later learned that ho had  edited footage of him into at least a  dozen additional ninja films each more  baffling than the last hendrix wrote of  ho in these fists break bricks he always  wanted more more ninjas more explosions  more everything as he said to his actors  i can't see her acting more acting ifd  later turned its attention from ninjas  to kickboxing after the success of  canon's bloodsport starring jean-claude  van damme ho later parted ways with his  ift partners and worked in films with  cynthia rothrock among others all while  enjoying cult status among fans of truly  bizarre cinema  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  martial arts movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

